Business user types:
 
“Show total sales by region for last quarter where revenue > 1M”
 
Your system:
Understands intent
Converts it into a safe SQL query
Executes it on your database
Shows results in UI
Allows Excel download
 
[ UI (Web App) ]
        |
        v
[ Backend API (Spring Boot) ]
        |
        |---> Schema + Metadata
        |---> LLM (Text → SQL)
        |
        v
[ Query Validator ]
        |
        v
[ Database (Read-only) ]
        |
        v
[ Result Formatter ]
        |
        |---> JSON (UI)
        |---> Excel (Download)
 
 
🔹 Step 1: User input (Frontend)
 
User enters plain English:
"List top 10 customers by revenue in 2024"
Frontend sends request:
{
  "question": "List top 10 customers by revenue in 2024"
}
 
🔹 Step 2: Backend receives request
 
Spring Boot controller:
POST /api/query
Backend responsibilities:
Authenticate user
Log request
Fetch database metadata
Call LLM
 
🔹 Step 3: Database schema awareness (CRITICAL)
 
You must not let the LLM guess your schema.
 
Create a Schema Registry:
Table names
Column names
Relationships
Business descriptions
 
Example (simplified):
{
  "tables": [
    {
      "name": "orders",
      "description": "Customer orders",
      "columns": [
        {"name": "order_id", "type": "int"},
        {"name": "customer_id", "type": "int"},
        {"name": "order_date", "type": "date"},
        {"name": "revenue", "type": "decimal"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "customers",
      "columns": [
        {"name": "customer_id", "type": "int"},
        {"name": "customer_name", "type": "string"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
Store this:
In code
Or in a metadata DB
Or cache it in Redis
 
🔹 Step 4: Prompt LLM to generate SQL
 
This is the heart of the system.
 
You do NOT ask:
 
“Give me SQL”
 
You ask:
 
“Generate SAFE, READ-ONLY SQL using ONLY these tables”
 
Example prompt (simplified):
You are a SQL generator.
Rules:
- Use ONLY the provided schema
- Generate SELECT queries only
- Do NOT use DELETE, UPDATE, INSERT
- Do NOT hallucinate tables or columns
- If the question cannot be answered, say "NOT_POSSIBLE"
Schema:
<schema_json>
User question:
"List top 10 customers by revenue in 2024"
LLM output:
SELECT c.customer_name, SUM(o.revenue) AS total_revenue
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE YEAR(o.order_date) = 2024
GROUP BY c.customer_name
ORDER BY total_revenue DESC
LIMIT 10;
 
🔹 Step 5: SQL validation & safety checks (MANDATORY)
 
Before execution:
 
✔ Allow only:
SELECT
JOIN
WHERE
GROUP BY
ORDER BY
LIMIT
 
❌ Reject if:
UPDATE / DELETE / DROP
Subqueries on system tables
Multiple statements
Comments or injections
 
You can:
Use a SQL parser (JSqlParser)
Regex as a secondary check
 
Also:
Use a read-only DB user
Enforce row limits
 
🔹 Step 6: Execute query
 
Execute via:
JDBC
Spring Data
NamedParameterJdbcTemplate
 
Return result as:
{
  "columns": ["customer_name", "total_revenue"],
  "rows": [
    ["Acme Corp", 1240000],
    ["Globex", 980000]
  ]
}
 
🔹 Step 7: Optional LLM explanation (UX bonus)
 
You can ask the LLM:
 
“Explain this result in plain English”
 
Example:
 
“Acme Corp generated the highest revenue in 2024 with 1.24M.”
 
This builds trust with business users.
 
Excel download flow
 
🔹 Backend
 
Use:
Apache POI
Or EasyExcel
 
Process:
Take query result
Create .xlsx
Stream it as response
 
Spring endpoint:
GET /api/query/{id}/download
Headers:
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="result.xlsx"
 
🔹 Frontend
Button: Download Excel
Calls download endpoint
Browser handles file
 
LLM selection & strategy
 
✅ Recommended approach
GPT-4 / GPT-4o or Claude
Low temperature (0–0.2)
Strict system prompt
JSON-only responses (for SQL + metadata)
 
🔁 Retry logic
 
If SQL fails:
Send error back to LLM:
“This query failed due to column X not found. Fix it.”
